{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"references","title":"References in Depth","group":"Pointers, Arrays & Strings","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A reference is another name for an existing object. C++ has lvalue references (<code>T&amp;</code>), const references (<code>const T&amp;</code>) and rvalue references (<code>T&amp;&amp;</code>).</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>lvalue references</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    int x = 1;\n    int&amp; r = x;          // r is x\n    r = 5;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (&amp;r == &amp;x) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    int y = 99;\n    r = y;               // NOT re-binding: assigns 99 to x\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 2, 3};\n    int&amp; first = v[0];   // reference to an element\n    first = 100;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v[0] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 1\n99\n100</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li>Must be initialised: <code>int&amp; r;</code> is an error.</li><li>Can't be null and can't be re-seated to another object.</li><li>No “reference to reference”, no arrays of references, no pointers to references.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>const references bind to temporaries</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nstd::string makeName() { return \"temp\"; }\nint main() {\n    const std::string&amp; ref = makeName();   // lifetime of the temporary is extended to ref's scope\n    const int&amp; n = 42;                     // binds to a materialised temporary\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; ref &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // std::string&amp; bad = makeName();      // error: non-const lvalue ref can't bind to an rvalue\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>temp 42</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-category\"></i>lvalues and rvalues</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>lvalue</strong> — an expression that names an object with an identity — you can take its address: variables, <code>*p</code>, <code>v[i]</code>, a function returning <code>T&amp;</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>rvalue</strong> — a temporary or literal with no persistent identity: <code>42</code>, <code>a + b</code>, <code>makeName()</code>, <code>std::move(x)</code>. Rvalue references <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> bind only to these — the basis of move semantics.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nvoid take(const std::string&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"lvalue (or const)\\n\"; }\nvoid take(std::string&amp;&amp;)      { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"rvalue — can steal its guts\\n\"; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::string s = \"hi\";\n    take(s);                     // lvalue\n    take(std::string(\"tmp\"));    // prvalue (temporary)\n    take(s + \"!\");               // result of an expression is a temporary\n    take(std::move(s));          // std::move casts s to an rvalue (xvalue)\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>lvalue (or const)\nrvalue — can steal its guts\nrvalue — can steal its guts\nrvalue — can steal its guts</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reference</th><th>Binds to lvalue</th><th>Binds to rvalue</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>T&amp;</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Modify caller's object</td></tr><tr><td><code>const T&amp;</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Read-only parameter, no copy</td></tr><tr><td><code>T&amp;&amp;</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Move constructors, sink parameters</td></tr><tr><td><code>auto&amp;&amp;</code> / <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> in a template</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Forwarding (“universal”) reference</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-unlink\"></i>Dangling references</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — reference invalidated by reallocation</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 2, 3};\n    int&amp; ref = v[0];\n    v.push_back(4);             // may reallocate: ref now refers to freed memory\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; ref &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>References don't keep objects alive (except the temporary-lifetime-extension case above). Returning a reference to a local, or holding one into a <code>vector</code> that grows, is undefined behaviour.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can a reference be null?</summary><div><p>Not in a valid program. You can <em>create</em> one by dereferencing a null pointer (<code>int&amp; r = *p;</code>), but that dereference is already undefined behaviour. Code may assume references always refer to an object.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does it mean that std::move doesn't move anything?</summary><div><p><code>std::move(x)</code> is just a cast to <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> — it marks <code>x</code> as an rvalue so that overload resolution picks the move constructor/assignment. The actual moving happens in that constructor. After being moved from, <code>x</code> is valid but in an unspecified state.</p></div></details></section>"}